From: Oever Gonzalez <software@notengobattery.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7996: detach the NPU from the chip before resetting it

Parking the offload path stops the NPU handing frames to the wireless
side, but it does not stop the NPU reaching the chip at all:
mt7996_npu_set_pcie_addr() and mt7996_npu_txrx_offload_init() give it
the PCIe addresses of the chip's ring registers, and it keeps using them.
So mt7996_dma_reset() pulls the wireless subsystem out from under a bus
master that is still addressing it, and the root port reports an
uncorrectable CmpltTO within a few hundred milliseconds -- long before
the firmware download that gets blamed for it in the log.

A module reload survives the same wfsys reset because it never performs
one against a bound NPU: unload clears mmio.npu and drops the device,
and the reset at re-probe happens before mt76_npu_init() has bound
anything.

mt7996_npu_hw_stop() already knows the command for this -- it sends
ifindex 4 with a zeroed INODE_TXRX_REG_ADDR, revoking the register
binding -- but it then polls GET_WAIT_NPU_INFO for zero, and on this
firmware that value reads 1 even on a quiescent device, so it always
returns -ETIMEDOUT and the caller discards a stop that did happen.

Send the revoke on its own, give in-flight transactions a moment to
land, and leave the polling out of it.

--- a/mt7996/npu.c
+++ b/mt7996/npu.c
@@ -525,6 +525,29 @@
 				 on, GFP_KERNEL);
 }
 
+int __mt7996_npu_detach(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
+{
+	struct airoha_npu *npu;
+	int err;
+
+	npu = rcu_dereference_protected(dev->mt76.mmio.npu, &dev->mt76.mutex);
+	if (!npu)
+		return 0;
+
+	/* revokes the inode's txrx register address: parking stops the frame
+	 * handoff, only this stops the NPU reaching the chip's ring registers
+	 */
+	err = mt76_npu_send_msg(npu, 4, WLAN_FUNC_SET_WAIT_INODE_TXRX_REG_ADDR,
+				0, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
+	/* let whatever is already in flight land before the chip goes away */
+	usleep_range(10000, 15000);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 int __mt7996_npu_park(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
 {
 	return mt7996_npu_force_to_cpu(dev, 1);
--- a/mt7996/mt7996.h
+++ b/mt7996/mt7996.h
@@ -1006,6 +1006,7 @@
 #ifdef CONFIG_MT7996_NPU
 int __mt7996_npu_hw_init(struct mt7996_dev *dev);
 int __mt7996_npu_mem_reinit(struct mt7996_dev *dev);
+int __mt7996_npu_detach(struct mt7996_dev *dev);
 int __mt7996_npu_park(struct mt7996_dev *dev);
 int __mt7996_npu_unpark(struct mt7996_dev *dev);
 int mt7996_npu_hw_init(struct mt7996_dev *dev);
@@ -1021,6 +1022,11 @@
 {
 	return 0;
 }
+
+static inline int __mt7996_npu_detach(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
+{
+	return 0;
+}
 
 static inline int __mt7996_npu_park(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
 {
--- a/mt7996/mac.c
+++ b/mt7996/mac.c
@@ -2337,6 +2337,13 @@
 	if (err)
 		dev_err(dev->mt76.dev, "npu park failed: %d\n", err);
 
+	/* the wfsys reset below takes the chip out from under the NPU: the
+	 * reload survives it only because it unbinds the NPU first
+	 */
+	err = __mt7996_npu_detach(dev);
+	if (err)
+		dev_err(dev->mt76.dev, "npu detach failed: %d\n", err);
+
 	mt7996_dma_reset(dev, true);
 
 	mt76_for_each_q_rx(mdev, i) {
